<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panagyurishte Treasure</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Panagyurishte Treasure is a Thracian hoard of nine gold vessels found in 1949 near Panagyurishte in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Weighing over 6 kilograms, it includes a phiale, an amphora-rhyton and several animal-headed rhyta decorated with scenes from Greek myth, and dates from the late 4th or early 3rd century BCE. It is one of the finest surviving examples of Thracian elite metalwork.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panagyurishte-treasure.jpg" alt="Panagyurishte Treasure" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ann Wuyts · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sofia_-_Panagyurishte_Thracian_Gold_Treasure.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The treasure was found by chance on 8 December 1949 by three brothers, Pavel, Petko and Michail Deikov, who worked together at the Merul tile factory near the railway station of Panagyurishte. Peter Gorbanov, a curator at a local museum near the site, recognised the find as a collection of Thracian artefacts. At the time it was called the richest treasure to have been unearthed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. Later studies took up its details; John G. Griffith, for one, wrote in 1974 on the siege scene on the gold amphora.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>All nine vessels are of 24-karat gold, 6.164 kilograms in all, and all are richly and skilfully decorated. Beside the phiale and the amphora-rhyton the set holds seven rhyta. The phiale carries inscriptions giving its weight both in Greek drachmae and in Persian darics. The vessels are thought to have served as a royal ceremonial set of the Thracian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a>. They may have been buried to hide them during invasions of the area by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>As one of the best known surviving artefacts of Thracian culture, the treasure has been shown in museums around the world, among them the Metropolitan Museum of Art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, whose exhibition Thracian Treasures from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> ran from 11 June to 4 September 1977. At home it is the centrepiece of the Thracian art collections of the Plovdiv Regional Historical Museum, the National Museum of History in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> and the History Museum in Panagyurishte. Three replica sets are displayed in those museums whenever the original is lent for exhibitions abroad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Panagyurishte Treasure” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hoards such as the Rogozen treasure, more than 160 silver vessels, several inscribed with the names of Odrysian kings, and the gold <b>Panagyurishte</b> treasure of the late fourth or early third century BCE show the scale of elite display.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
